.TH GIT-WORKFLOW-BRANCH 1 2026-10-25 git-workflow "git-workflow manual"
.SH NAME
git-workflow-branch \- start, prerelease, release, finish and delete branches following the flow
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git workflow branch
.RI [ git-branch-options ]
.br
.B git workflow branch start
.I type
.RI [ name ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B git workflow branch pre
.RI [ label | vX.Y.Z | tag ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B git workflow branch release
.RI [ vX.Y.Z ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B git workflow branch finish
.RI [ branch ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.br
.B git workflow branch delete
.RI [ branch ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.br
.B git workflow branch config
.RB [ \-\-types ]
|
.B help
|
.B version
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no subcommand, or with options
.RB ( \-a ,
.BR \-vv ,
.B \-d
.IR branch ,
\&...),
.B git workflow branch
runs
.BR git\-branch (1)
with the same arguments.
Any other word is refused as an unknown subcommand, so a mistyped one
.RB ( "finsh" )
is not quietly created as a branch; use
.B git branch
.I name
to create a branch outside the flow.
.PP
With the default aliases
.RB ( "git workflow aliases install" )
this is also
.BR "git b" ;
the 1.x name
.B b
works after
.B git workflow
too.
.PP
.B start ,
.B pre ,
.B release
and
.B finish
take
.B \-y
.RB ( \-\-yes ):
answer their questions with yes, printing each one followed by
.BR "y (\-y)" ,
so they can run from a script. Flow warnings are not questions
.B \-y
answers: they still ask, and abort without a terminal or when
.B workflow.strict
is set. Where a question offers to roll back a failed
.BR finish ,
yes is the rollback.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start \fItype\fR [\fIname\fR] [\fB\-y\fR]
Create a branch of one of the flow's types from where the type says, and
push it.
.B config
lists the types. In
.BR gitflow ,
topic branches start from the dev branch;
.I release/vX.Y.Z
from the dev branch;
.I hotfix/vX.Y.Z
from the main branch. In
.BR github ,
topic branches start from the main branch and
.I hotfix/vX.Y.Z
from the latest release tag, since the main branch already has unreleased
work. Versions are checked against existing tags. The 2.x spellings
.RB ( t ,
.BR \-\-topic ,
\&...) still work.
Tracked edits come along, as with
.BR "git switch \-c" .
A name that already exists locally or on the remote is refused.
.TP
.B pre \fR[\fIlabel\fR | \fIvX.Y.Z\fR | \fItag\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-l\fR] [\fB\-y\fR]
Tag the current release or hotfix branch (any versioned type) as a
prerelease and push the tag.
With no argument the tag is
.IR vX.Y.Z\-rc.N ,
numbered after the existing ones; a
.I label
replaces
.BR rc ;
.I vX.Y.Z
names the version; a full
.I tag
is used as given.
Unpushed branch commits are pushed first (after asking); a branch that is
behind its remote is refused.
In flows that release from the main branch
.RB ( workflow.releaseFromMain ,
set by
.BR github ),
it also works there: it fast-forwards the main branch first, takes the
version from
.I vX.Y.Z
or the
.B workflow.versionFile
file, and refuses local commits the remote main branch doesn't have, since
only the tag is pushed.
.B \-n
shows what would be tagged and never prompts or stops.
.B \-l
lists the prereleases for the branch's version.
.TP
.B release \fR[\fIvX.Y.Z\fR] [\fB\-n\fR] [\fB\-y\fR]
In flows that release from the main branch: tag the commit the newest
prerelease of
.I vX.Y.Z
tested as
.I vX.Y.Z
and push only the tag, so it works when the main branch only accepts pull
requests. With no version, the newest prerelease that hasn't been
released yet is promoted. The commit must be on the remote main branch;
commits the main branch gained after it are reported and go in the next
release. With no prerelease the release is untested: it warns, and
.B workflow.strict
refuses.
.B \-n
shows what would be tagged.
.TP
.B finish \fR[\fIbranch\fR] [\fB\-y\fR]
Integrate a branch where its type says
.RB ( config
shows where).
.IP
With pull requests
.RB ( workflow.integrate=pr ,
the default), the branch is pushed and a pull request opened (or the open
one shown). Run
.B finish
again once it's merged: a topic branch is deleted, however it was merged,
and the dev branch brought up to date. A release or hotfix branch is
tagged
.I vX.Y.Z
and only the tag pushed; in
.BR gitflow ,
a pull request from the main branch into the dev branch follows. Tagging
the merge works however the pull request was merged; tagging the tested
tip (GitHub flow's hotfixes) needs a merge commit, and a squashed one is
refused.
.IP
Merging locally
.RB ( workflow.integrate=merge ),
a topic branch is merged into the dev branch with
.BR \-\-no\-ff ,
pushed, and deleted. A release or hotfix branch is merged into the main
branch, tagged
.I vX.Y.Z
(on the merge, or on the tested tip), merged back into the dev branch
(git flow), and pushed with
.B git push \-\-atomic
together with that one tag.
Nothing is pushed until every local step has succeeded. If a merge
conflicts, the dev branch has diverged, or the push is rejected, finish
restores the main and dev branches, deletes the tag it made, and returns
to the branch being finished, so it can simply be run again. For a
rejected release push or a back-merge conflict it asks first, and
otherwise prints the command that completes the release by hand.
.IP
Either way, commits after the last prerelease are flagged.
.TP
.B delete \fR[\fIbranch\fR] [\fB\-y\fR] [\fB\-f\fR]
Delete a branch locally and on the remote.
.B \-y
skips the question, and only applies when the branch is named.
A branch with commits that aren't merged into the main or dev branch
(locally or on the remote copy) is refused unless
.B \-f
is given; one whose pull request was squash- or rebase-merged counts as
merged. Protected branches are never deleted.
The 1.x spellings of
.B \-y
.RB ( \-d ,
.BR \-\-d ,
.BR \-\-delete ,
.BR \-\-y ,
and
.BR y ,
.B yes
or
.B d
after the branch name) still work, with a warning, until v4.
.TP
.B config \fR[\fB\-\-types\fR]
Show the flow, why it's the flow, every setting with where it came from,
and the branch types.
.B \-\-types
prints only the type names.
.TP
.B version
Show the installed version.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
git workflow branch start release v1.2.0
git workflow branch pre              # v1.2.0\-rc.1, pushed; CI publishes a prerelease
git workflow branch pre              # v1.2.0\-rc.2
git workflow branch finish           # pull request into main; once it's merged,
git workflow branch finish           # v1.2.0 tagged, back-merge pull request into next

# GitHub flow
git workflow branch start topic fix
git workflow branch finish           # pushes it, opens a pull request
git workflow branch finish           # after the merge: deletes the branch
git workflow branch pre              # on main: v1.3.0\-rc.1 (from VERSION)
git workflow branch release          # tags rc.1's commit v1.3.0
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git\-workflow (1)
for configuration and guardrails.
